**Which of the following is not an indication for urgent neurosurgical intervention in a patient with pituitary apoplexy?**

A 41-year-old woman presented to the accident and emergency department with a history of sudden-onset severe headache, nausea, vomiting, and visual disturbance. On examination, she was apyrexial, hypotensive, and tachycardic, with no features of meningism. She also had evidence of third, fourth, and sixth cranial nerve palsies. MRI was arranged (see Fig. 1.3).

{{image}}

**Which one of the following is a correct statement regarding the management of pituitary apoplexy?**

